## Summary

`dsh` (DeepSeek Harness) crashes with a fatal error and the whole process exits when a user skill directory (e.g. `~/.agents/skills`) contains **circular junctions** (directory A points to B while B points back to A). A single corrupted directory should never take down the entire service.

## Environment

- dsh version: `0.1.0-rc.6` (package `@deepseek-ai/dsh-skill-filesystem`)
- OS: Windows 11 (NTFS junction points; same class of problem exists for symlinks on macOS/Linux)
- Trigger: startup scan of `~/.agents/skills` by the `skill-filesystem` provider

## Actual behavior

dsh starts, listens on the port, then the Node.js process dies shortly after with:

```
dsh: fatal load failure: Error: ELOOP: too many symbolic links encountered, stat 'C:\Users\56891\.agents\skills\cli-anything-wps'
    at async Object.stat (node:internal/fs/promises:1038:18)
    at async NodeFsHandler._addToNodeFs (.../chokidar/handler.js:580:27)
```

The browser then fails to connect to the GUI (`failed to fetch`).

## Root cause

The skill watcher in `dsh-skill-filesystem` (`lib/index.js`) creates a chokidar watcher with `followSymlinks: this.config.followSymlinks` (default `true`, `watchFollowSymlinks` config):

```js
const watcher = chokidar.watch(mode.anchor, {
    persistent: true,
    ignoreInitial: true,
    depth: 1,
    followSymlinks: this.config.followSymlinks,
    ...
});
```

When the watched root contains circular junctions, chokidar follows the cycle and hits `ELOOP`. The error handler wired *before* the watcher is ready rejects the readiness promise, and that rejection is **rethrown** up the stack:

```js
const onError = (error) => {
    if (!ready) {
        readiness.reject(error);   // <-- ELOOP during initial scan
        return;
    }
    this.handleWatcherError(state, error);
};
...
try {
    await readiness.promise;
} catch (error) {
    await this.closeWatcher(handle);
    throw error;                   // <-- propagates -> fatal
}
```

`replaceWatcher` also warns but **rethrows**:

```js
} catch (error) {
    if (!this.closing) {
        state.unhealthy = true;
        this.ctx.logger.warn(`skill-filesystem: failed to watch ${state.root.path}: ${errorMessage(error)}`);
    }
    throw error;                   // <-- fatal
}
```

So an `ELOOP` during the initial scan escapes as a fatal load failure instead of being contained per-directory.

Reproduction (Windows):

```powershell
# create two skill dirs that point at each other
New-Item -ItemType Junction -Path "$HOME\.agents\skills\a" -Target "$HOME\.claude\skills\a"
New-Item -ItemType Junction -Path "$HOME\.claude\skills\a" -Target "$HOME\.agents\skills\a"
# start dsh -> process exits with ELOOP fatal
```

## Expected behavior

A broken/unreadable skill directory (circular junction, permission error, or any per-directory failure) should:

1. be logged as a `WARNING` (e.g. `WARNING: skipping unreadable skill dir ...`) and skipped;
2. not abort the skill scan for the remaining roots;
3. never terminate the dsh process.

## Suggested fix

- Route pre-`ready` watcher errors through the same degradation path as `handleWatcherError` (warn + mark root `unhealthy` + skip/retry) instead of rejecting the readiness promise and rethrowing.
- Special-case `ELOOP`/`ELOOP`-class errors: drop the affected root from the watch set, emit a warning, and optionally re-probe on the next invalidation cycle.
- Consider detecting cycles during discovery (track visited realpaths per root) so the scan can prune cyclic entries before chokidar follows them.
- Optional hardening: make `watchFollowSymlinks` default to `false` for user-level roots, or document the risk.

## Notes

- This was hit in the wild: a third-party "sync" tool had converted both sides of `~/.agents/skills` and `~/.claude/skills` into junctions pointing at each other; dsh died on every startup until the cycles were manually removed.
- Same failure mode applies to any circular symlink/junction under a watched skill root.

