> Unofficial project, independently developed and maintained by community members.

## What it is

**[dsh-quota-router](https://github.com/Liyuk/dsh-quota-router) 1.0.0** is a policy-only multi-source model routing and failure-recovery plugin for DeepSeek Harness (DSH).

It selects among provider/model routes already registered and natively validated by DSH. It does not manage providers or credentials, and it does not implement tool lazy-loading, context compaction, or model-quality evaluation.

## 1.0 capabilities

- Task Profile matching with first-match semantics;
- Global source priority × per-task model mapping to build ordered candidate chains;
- Native DSH provider/model validation;
- Explicit paid opt-in; manual/emergency sources are never auto-selected;
- Stable failures advance immediately, while transient failures first use DSH retry;
- Forward-only fallback with cooldown;
- Per-turn route identity, bounded idempotent receipts, and an in-memory ledger;
- DSH Web Settings → Quota Router configuration and candidate-chain preview;
- SubtaskRouter with explicit capability constraints and model leases;
- quota_router_status and session Route Receipt observability.

## How it integrates with DSH

Install:

```bash
npm install @liyuk/dsh-quota-router
```

Register providers/models in DSH Models first. Then configure source priority, task Profiles, model mappings, paid-fallback protection, transient thresholds, and cooldown in Settings → Quota Router.

The router only chooses candidates that DSH already knows and accepts. It does not replace the Agent Loop or create a second session store.

## Cost and scope

The plugin can reduce unnecessary provider calls by using appropriate routes, skipping known-bad candidates, and avoiding repeated retries of stable failures. It does **not** claim guaranteed token savings or guaranteed lower total cost; evaluate route cost together with quality, rework, and a comparable baseline.

Route cost and context cost are separate:

- route cost: provider/model, input/output/cache/reasoning tokens, fallback;
- context cost: tool schemas, prompts, cache, compaction, and context visibility.

## Links

- Project: https://github.com/Liyuk/dsh-quota-router
- npm: https://www.npmjs.com/package/@liyuk/dsh-quota-router
- Release/community notes: https://github.com/Liyuk/dsh-quota-router/blob/main/docs/RELEASE-1.0.0.md
- Architecture diagram: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Liyuk/dsh-quota-router/main/docs/assets/architecture.svg

Feedback is welcome on Profile matching, fallback recovery, DSH adapter compatibility, and composition with context/tooling plugins.

The DSH ecosystem now has enough plugins that discovering them is easy, but choosing a coherent set is becoming real work. Users have to resolve feature overlap, license boundaries, version drift, platform differences, and installation guidance before they can run their first useful task.

I have published **DeepSeek Harness Ultimate**, a community-maintained profile installer that turns those choices into a public, reproducible manifest.

Key decisions:

- one default per overlapping responsibility;
- full commit-SHA pins for selected upstream sources;
- recorded permissive licenses with notices preserved;
- dependency preflight and installed-tree audits;
- Windows, macOS, and Linux setup paths;
- paired README and beginner tutorials in 20 languages;
- credential- and permission-heavy integrations kept optional.

It is not an official DeepSeek AI release and does not bundle every plugin. The aim is curated coverage of nearly every practical, publicly auditable capability category without installing duplicate schedulers, planners, or team orchestrators.

Project: https://github.com/18126295767-cell/deepseek-harness-ultimate

I would especially appreciate feedback from DSH maintainers and plugin authors on the composition rules, attribution, and any category where a selected default has a clearer maintained alternative.

