- 如果社区认为值得演进，上述方案改动很小（约 25–30 行、5 个函数）。
- 也欢迎指出我对机制理解的偏差，或补充我遗漏的边界情况。

### Symptom

`SessionProjectionRegistry` schema validation fails with:

```json
[{ "origin": "number", "code": "too_small", "minimum": 0, "inclusive": true, "path": ["messageTokens"], "message": "Too small: expected number to be >=0" }]
```

Timeline: model context reaches the window → compaction runs → the model finishes the session → every subsequent message send reports this error. The compressed range's token accounting leaves `messageTokens` negative, and the projected state no longer satisfies `z.number().int().nonnegative()`.

### Root cause

`drive()` in `packages/session/session-projection/src/index.ts` had two ordering defects:

1. It committed the projection state `cell.state = next` BEFORE running `schema.parse(...)`.
2. It only validated when listeners were attached (`if (changed && this.listeners.size > 0)`).

Consequences:
- With no listener attached (headless runs), a schema-violating transition (negative `messageTokens` from a compaction summary/prune claim) is silently persisted into the cell.
- Because `cell.state` is written before parsing, even a listener-attached throw leaves the invalid state already committed.
- The error only surfaces later on an unconditional read path (`snapshot()`, `restore()`, `viewCheckpoint()`), by which point the session is broken for every subsequent message.

The session-event observer swallows listener throws (`invokeContainedSessionObservers` wraps each callback in try/catch and logs), so `session.append` does not surface the failure. The correct behavior is to NOT commit the invalid transition and keep serving the last valid state.

### Fix

Apply validation on every changed transition BEFORE committing state, matching the read paths:

```ts
private drive(session: Session, event: SessionEvent): void {
  for (const registration of this.registrations.values()) {
    let cell = registration.cells.get(session)
    if (cell === undefined) {
      cell = this.buildCell(registration.def, session.events.slice(0, event.seq))
      registration.cells.set(session, cell)
    }
    const next = registration.def.apply(cell.state, event)
    const changed = !Object.is(next, cell.state)
    if (changed) {
      // Validate the unit's wire payload BEFORE committing its state, so a
      // schema-violating transition never lands in the cell.
      const value = registration.def.schema.parse(registration.def.view(next))
      cell.state = next
      for (const listener of this.listeners) {
        listener(session, registration.def.key, value, event.seq)
      }
    }
    cell.observedSeq = event.seq
  }
}
```

Key change: `schema.parse` now runs for every changed transition (listener or not) and runs BEFORE `cell.state = next`. A schema-violating transition is not committed; the cell keeps serving the last valid value, and `session.append` returns normally (observer swallows the throw as designed).

Hi! I noticed this while reading through `subagent-codex` to understand how DSH turns the Codex App Server event stream into a one-shot `SubagentRun`.

The overall choice makes sense to me: the adapter ignores intermediate noise, waits for the authoritative `item/completed` / `turn/completed` events, and returns only the final agent answer to the parent. While tracing that final-answer selection, I came across a small wire-compatibility edge case that may be worth confirming.

## What the adapter does today

For a completed `agentMessage`, the adapter:

- prefers `phase: "final_answer"`;
- uses `phase: null` as the compatibility fallback;
- ignores `phase: "commentary"`;
- rejects any other value.

The relevant code is here: [wire.ts](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/master/packages/subagent/subagent-codex/src/wire.ts#L347-L352)

```ts
if (item.phase === 'final_answer') {
  this.lastFinalAnswer = text
} else if (item.phase === null) {
  this.lastUnphasedAnswer = text
} else if (item.phase !== 'commentary') {
  throw new Error(...)
}
```

If the `phase` property is omitted entirely, however, `item.phase` becomes `undefined` and falls into the unknown-phase error.

## Why I am asking rather than calling it a bug

The current Codex App Server documentation describes an agent message as:

```ts
{ id, text, phase? }
```

https://developers.openai.com/codex/app-server

That suggests the field can be absent. At the same time, DSH currently pins `@openai/codex` 0.147.0, and I have not confirmed whether that exact version always serializes an unphased message as `phase: null`.

So this may be a theoretical compatibility gap rather than a reproduced runtime regression. There may also be an intentional reason to require the field from the pinned protocol version that I have missed.

## Small possible change

If omission is meant to be equivalent to an unphased message, the fallback could accept both representations:

```ts
} else if (item.phase === null || item.phase === undefined) {
  this.lastUnphasedAnswer = text
}
```

Unknown non-null values such as a future unsupported phase would still fail closed.

The scope could stay local to `subagent-codex`:

- cover `null`, omitted phase, explicit `final_answer`, and an unknown phase in the wire tests;
- update the English and Chinese README wording;
- leave the public Subagent interfaces and lifecycle semantics unchanged.

Would accepting an omitted phase here match the intended compatibility policy for the pinned App Server protocol? If so, I would be happy to prepare the exact patch for review in whichever form is most useful.

## Summary

Tool calls from several models are rejected with `Error: invalid arguments: missing required property "description"` (a `ToolArgsError`, code `INVALID_ARGS`) because the `bash` and `run_code` tools declare `description` as a **required** argument:

- `dsh-tool-bash`: `parameters.description.required = true` (lib/index.js)
- `dsh-tools` `run_code`: `parameters.description.required = true` (`createRunCodeTool`)

`description` is a UI-annotation field — it is never used to decide what runs. The safety-relevant fields (`command`, `code`) are already required and untouched by this proposal.

## Observed behavior

Since running sessions on OpenRouter free-tier models (`nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free`, `poolside/laguna-s-2.1:free`) and deepseek v4-pro/flash, the harness rejects a steady stream of otherwise-valid calls:

```
Error: invalid arguments: missing required property "description"
```

Session logs show a typical `tool/result` event:

```json
{
  "type": "tool/result",
  "data": {
    "message": {
      "content": [{
        "type": "tool-result",
        "content": [{
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Error: invalid arguments: missing required property \"description\""
        }],
        "isError": true
      }]
    },
    "error": { "name": "ToolArgsError", "code": "INVALID_ARGS" }
  }
}
```

In one session: 14 such rejections (7 on deepseek-v4-pro, 7 on the free-tier model), all `run_code` calls carrying only `code` — the inner `tools.bash`/`tools.edit` calls had their own descriptions and were fine. The model repeated the exact same omission up to **4 consecutive times** before correcting, so each rejected call costs extra tokens and clutters the transcript. A session run entirely on another model (141 tool calls) had zero rejections, confirming this is model adherence, not a harness fault.

Verified unchanged between `0.1.0-rc.6` and `0.1.1-rc.2` (parameter blocks and validator byte-identical).

## Suggestion

Treat `description` as **optional with a placeholder default** (e.g. the tool name, or `(no description provided)`) on `bash`/`run_code` — or, if that is undesirable, add a light auto-repair step that injects a placeholder description only when that is the sole missing required property, and feeds the repaired call through. `command`/`code` stay mandatory either way, so nothing unsafe can slip through.

## Environment

- dsh `0.1.1-rc.2` (also reproduced on `0.1.0-rc.6`), web GUI
- Providers: OpenRouter (`:free` tier models), plus deepseek v4-pro/flash

## Environment
- dsh version: 0.1.0-rc.6 (also reproduced on 0.1.1-rc.2)
- Client: older mobile browser / Android WebView (Chromium < 116, no `AbortSignal.any`); browser version unchanged during the whole period
- Server: Windows 10, Node 24, `dsh web` on 127.0.0.1:3080

## Symptom
The Web UI loads and renders normally on the phone, but sending a chat message does nothing. Console shows:

```
AbortSignal.any is not a function
```

A desktop browser with a recent Chrome works fine.

## Root cause
`@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-connection/lib/client.js` calls `AbortSignal.any()` / `AbortSignal.timeout()` without feature detection in `postJson()` (the shared POST leg of the C→S RPC carrier). `AbortSignal.any` requires Chromium 116+ / Safari 17.4+, so on older mobile browsers/WebViews the very first RPC that carries a caller signal (i.e. sending a message) throws, breaking the chat composer while the rest of the UI keeps rendering. Passive calls without a caller signal only need `AbortSignal.timeout` (Chromium 103+), which is why browsing sessions still worked on the same browser.

## What I ruled out
- Third-party plugins (dshmarket / dsh-genui / dsh-at-file / dsh-better-sidebar / dsh-chat-import): their only `AbortSignal.any` uses are either host-side Node code (dshmarket `lib/gist.js`) or guarded with `typeof AbortSignal.any == 'function'` (dsh-genui's bundled three.js). None of their browser bundles call it unguarded.
- Network path: API trust fence, Host/Origin checks and WebSocket were all verified working; PC browser on the same server never had the issue.

## Remaining puzzle
On the same dsh version (0.1.0-rc.6) and the same phone browser, sending reportedly worked at first and broke later — possibly a stale cached frontend bundle masking the issue initially. Mentioning in case it hints at a second variable (bundle caching / service worker?).

## Workaround (confirmed working)
Injecting a small polyfill for `AbortSignal.any` / `AbortSignal.timeout` before the module entry in the served `index.html` fully restores mobile use:

```js
if (typeof AbortSignal !== 'undefined') {
  if (typeof AbortSignal.any !== 'function') {
    AbortSignal.any = function (signals) {
      var c = new AbortController();
      for (var i = 0; i < signals.length; i++) {
        (function (s) {
          if (s.aborted) { c.abort(s.reason); return; }
          s.addEventListener('abort', function () { c.abort(s.reason); }, { once: true });
        })(signals[i]);
      }
      return c.signal;
    };
  }
  if (typeof AbortSignal.timeout !== 'function') {
    AbortSignal.timeout = function (ms) {
      var c = new AbortController();
      setTimeout(function () { c.abort(new DOMException('The operation timed out.', 'TimeoutError')); }, ms);
      return c.signal;
    };
  }
}
```

## Suggestion
Ship the polyfill at build time, or feature-detect and fall back, so older mobile browsers keep working.

