### Summary

`client-hmr` opens its `/plugins/events` channel with `EventSource` (SSE). SSE is a long-lived **HTTP** connection, so it consumes one slot from the browser's per-origin HTTP connection pool and never releases it. Every DSH frontend takes one such slot.

Once a page runs several DSH frontends against the same origin, the pool is exhausted and any further frontend can no longer even fetch its own document — its navigation request stays `pending` forever and the UI sits at **"Loading plugins…"** indefinitely. It never errors out, because nothing failed: the request is queued behind a queue that never drains.

Switching that one channel from SSE to WebSocket removes the ceiling entirely, because WebSocket connections are governed by a separate, far more permissive browser quota.

### Environment

- DSH `0.1.0-rc.7` and `0.1.1-rc.2` (both reproduce; the relevant code is byte-identical in the two versions)
- Windows 11, Node 24
- Chrome (loopback origin, plain HTTP → always HTTP/1.1, so the 6-connections-per-origin rule applies)

### Reproduction

1. Open a page that embeds several DSH frontends against the same origin — e.g. an HTML shell with N `<iframe src="/?…">`, or simply several tabs on the same origin.
2. Beyond a small N (2–3 in our environment, depending on how many frontends the origin already carries), the newly added frontends stop loading and stay on "Loading plugins…".
3. DevTools shows their **main document request stuck in `pending`**, and **no new TCP connection is created** for them.

The server is entirely healthy while this happens — in our measurements it answered in 1–3 ms and fetching all 39 client modules serially took 1826 ms with zero failures.

### Root cause

Each DSH frontend holds exactly **3** long-lived connections:

| # | Source | Quota it consumes |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `dsh-client-connection` → `readWebSocket(MUX_EVENTS_PATH, …)` | WebSocket quota (permissive) |
| 2 | `dsh-client-connection` → `readWebSocket(HOST_EVENTS_PATH, …)` | WebSocket quota (permissive) |
| 3 | `dsh-client-hmr` → `new EventSource(EVENTS_ENDPOINT)` | **HTTP quota (6 per origin)** |

Only #3 is the problem. `packages/client/hmr` — `lib/client.js`:

```js
const source = new EventSource(EVENTS_ENDPOINT);
```

and the server half registers it as a plain HTTP route:

```js
ctx.webServer.register({ kind: "exact", path: EVENTS_ENDPOINT, handler: … });
// res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "text/event-stream", … })
```

Because this is HTTP/1.1 over a loopback origin (no HTTP/2 available — the web server is built on `node:http`), those SSE streams each pin one of the six per-origin HTTP slots for as long as the frontend lives.

### Evidence

Measured connection counts (each frontend consistently accounts for exactly 3 connections — opening one adds 3, closing one removes 3):

- **Before the change**: with 30 established connections, the 4th iframe on the origin could not start. Its document request was `pending` with no new connection attempted. Closing one existing frontend (freeing 3 connections) caused the stalled iframe to immediately open 3 connections and proceed — a clean causal loop.
- **After switching hmr to WebSocket**: **9 full DSH frontends in a single page, all rendering normally**, at the same 30 established connections. Identical connection count, opposite outcome — the difference is purely which quota pool those connections draw from.

### Suggested fix

Move the hmr channel to WebSocket. The infrastructure is already in the codebase: `ws` is a dependency of `dsh-client-connection`, and `webServer` already exposes `registerUpgrade` — the two existing WebSocket channels are mounted exactly this way. The change is roughly a dozen lines on each half.

Server (`packages/client/hmr`, node half):

```js
import { WebSocketServer } from "ws";

const wss = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true });
const connect = (socket) => {
  socket.send(JSON.stringify({ type: "graph", graph: ctx.clientModules.graph() }));
  connections.add(socket);
  socket.on("close", () => connections.delete(socket));
  socket.on("error", () => connections.delete(socket));
};

ctx.effect(() => {
  const disposeRoute = ctx.webServer.registerUpgrade({
    path: EVENTS_ENDPOINT,
    handler: (req, socket, head) => wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, connect),
  });
  const unsubscribe = ctx.clientModules.onRebuilt((id, rev) => {
    const line = JSON.stringify({ type: "rebuilt", id, rev });
    for (const socket of connections) socket.send(line);
  });
  return () => { /* unsubscribe / disposeRoute / close sockets / wss.close() */ };
}, "client-hmr: /plugins/events channel");
```

Browser (`packages/client/hmr`, client half):

```js
const wsUrl = new URL(EVENTS_ENDPOINT, location.href);
wsUrl.protocol = wsUrl.protocol === "https:" ? "wss:" : "ws:";
const source = new WebSocket(wsUrl);
```

The `message` listener and `close()` teardown below it need no changes at all — the two APIs agree on both. The wire protocol is unchanged apart from dropping the SSE `data: …\n\n` envelope.

### Verification

We applied exactly this change locally against `0.1.1-rc.2` and confirmed:

- **9 full DSH frontends in one page, all rendering correctly** (chat ×2, trajectory ×2, tool details ×2, composer ×2, plus a sidebar) — previously 1–2.
- **HMR still works end to end**: editing a client bundle produced 9 reload log lines within the same second, one per frontend. Detection, push over WebSocket, and module reload all function identically.

### Why this matters beyond our case

Anyone embedding more than one DSH frontend on a single origin hits this — split-pane workspaces, dashboards, comparison views, or simply a few tabs. The symptom (an indefinite "Loading plugins…" with a healthy server) gives no hint about the real cause, which makes it expensive to diagnose.

It also gets worse over time within a session: connections from closed pages were observed lingering for 15+ minutes, so a developer who has been opening and closing DSH tabs can hit the ceiling with fewer live frontends than expected.

`dsh web` announces readiness on success and says nothing on failure, so a host waiting on the `dsh web:` line cannot tell a plugin tree that **failed** from one that is still **loading**.

In `packages/bundle/web-app/src/index.ts`:

```ts
const settled = ctx.get('loader')?.await()
if (settled === undefined) announceReady()
else {
  void settled.then(() => {
    if (ctx.get('webServer') !== undefined) announceReady()
  // Loader reports a failed boot; this row only stays quiet.
  }, () => {})
}
```

## Why the reasoning behind the empty handler does not fully hold

The comment is right that `app-boot` awaits the same Loader and throws `<bin>: plugin tree failed to load: <detail>`, so on the CLI path the failure *is* reported and the process exits non-zero. The handler is declining to print it twice, not hiding it.

But that diagnostic is not always the observer's. A tree built **outside** `boot()` — which is the shape an embedding host uses — leaves this row the only place the rejection lands, and there it is discarded.

## What it costs a host

`dsh web: <url>` is the right readiness signal: `ctx.webServer.port` is readable before `settled` resolves, so a host that polls the port instead can hand its WebView an origin whose plugin tree is still loading. But announcing only on success makes "failed" and "still loading" the same observation — no line either way — so an embedding host must either treat its own timeout as fatal, turning *starts without some plugins* into *does not start*, or proceed and serve a harness quietly missing them.

The argument for silence is "no URL for a process that is about to exit". That is correct about the **URL** and says nothing about whether the failure should be **named**.

## Fix

Announce readiness exactly once, positively or not at all:

```
dsh web: readiness failed: <reason>
```

The disposal branch is worth naming too — settling after the webserver has gone is a real state and is currently indistinguishable from the same silence.

Applied in a fork at `d582942a2b`, on stderr with the same `dsh web:` prefix so a supervisor merging streams keeps its existing parser, and with the reason kept short because `app-boot` remains the authority on why. Happy to send it as a PR.

## Summary

The public `@deepseek-ai/dsh-web-app` bundle mounts `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-presets` and configures `default: standard`, but the shipped preset files lived under the CLI application and only the CLI launcher patched that private path into the roster.

An embedding host that consumes the Web bundle through the public profile contract therefore loads the plugin and UI successfully while exposing an empty preset roster.

## Reproduction

1. Build an embedding host around `@deepseek-ai/dsh-web-app`.
2. Load the Web profile bundle through `@deepseek-ai/dsh-app-boot` without launching the `@deepseek-ai/dsh` CLI package.
3. Open the Web UI and create a chat session.

## Actual behavior

Session creation fails with:

    agent-presets: preset "standard" not found (available: none)

The Plugins UI reports the roster plugin as mounted, which makes this particularly difficult to diagnose.

## Expected behavior

Built-in agent presets should be a shared runtime resource, independent of CLI, Web, or a future TUI. A profile bundle should be able to select that resource package and its default preset regardless of npm or pnpm installation layout.

## Root cause

The dependency graph installs the roster plugin, but preset compositions are filesystem assets rather than plugin modules. Their location was a private CLI assembly fact in `apps/cli/config/agent-presets`.

## Revised proposed fix

A tested implementation is available here:

https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/compare/master...boqiang:deepseek-harness:fix/web-bundle-agent-presets

Latest architecture commit:

https://github.com/boqiang/deepseek-harness/commit/e774dee9e

The original proposal moved the files into the Web bundle. After reviewing the ownership boundary, I revised it so neither CLI nor Web owns application-neutral presets. The branch now:

- adds the shared `@deepseek-ai/dsh-builtin-presets` resource package;
- moves `standard`, `code`, `minimal`, and `cordis` into that package;
- makes the package own the complete runtime dependency closure of those compositions;
- extends `dsh.bundle.agentPresets` with an optional owning package;
- makes `loadProfile` resolve that package from the actual installation before patching the trusted roster root;
- makes the Web bundle depend on and select the shared package;
- removes the CLI-only private path patch;
- updates runtime-closure checks, Cordis config checks, tests, and bilingual documentation.

