## Proposed ownership model

This should not be an official-vs-third-party distinction, and a plugin should not declare itself optional. The profile that assembles the application knows whether that deployment requires a bundle.

`dsh.profile.bundles` remains the ordered layer list. An optional `recoverableBundles` subset selects failure handling:

- explicit subset: authoritative;
- legacy profile with no subset: dependency-managed bundles default recoverable, installation-owned bundles remain required;
- `dsh plugin add`: records a newly installed out-of-tree bundle as recoverable;
- an unlisted recoverable name: invalid configuration and fails before startup.

## Host behavior in the PoC

Each bundle patch layer is applied as a separate root Include/Loader transaction.

- A required layer rejection preserves the current fail-loud behavior and disposes the complete tree.
- A recoverable layer rejection keeps the preceding committed tree, records a structured fault, and continues with later layers.
- Only accepted bundle patches become the base for live user-patch recomposition, so HMR does not silently retry an isolated layer.
- Rows inserted by an accepted layer carry profile-owned startup provenance.

The activation audit also contains a marked row that imports unsuccessfully, rejects activation, or remains pending. An unmarked row remains fatal.

## Browser behavior in the PoC

The Host client-module graph projects only `failurePolicy: "recoverable"`; absence means required. Browser boot records a marked import/apply/pending failure and continues to mount the required `uiRenderer`. Required client failures keep the existing fatal boot page.

If the same client package has multiple qualifying Host instances, any required instance wins, including when that instance appears later through lifecycle updates.

## Why this still fits “everything is a plugin”

The distinction is not plugin versus core. The application shell, graph composer, UI renderer, and feature packages remain plugins or composition machinery over plugins. The new distinction is required versus recoverable membership selected by the profile that assembles one application. Core only provides the isolation and reporting mechanism.

